Chapter: TypeClassCompoundHead

  cites Foreword chapter Console

class Showable where
  to-text : a -> Text

instance Showable Integer where
  to-text (n) = "int " & show n

instance Showable (List Integer) where
  to-text (xs) = "ints " & show (list-length xs)

instance Showable (List Text) where
  to-text (xs) = "texts " & show (list-length xs)

Section: Body

 COMPILER-33, the compound half. `parse-instance-type-head` returned the
 FIRST token inside a parenthesized instance head and skipped the rest, so
 `instance Showable (List Integer)` arrived as the bare token `List` with
 its argument gone, and the dictionary and the specialized method were
 keyed on `List` alone.

 THE TREE HAS NEVER SPELLED A SECOND COMPOUND HEAD. A census over 3,790
 files on 2026-09-01 found 16 instance declarations and exactly one
 compound head, so the collision below was unreachable by construction and
 no green suite could have reported it (L-CONSTRUCT). This chapter is that
 second head.

 `List Integer` and `List Text` both keyed to `List`, so the two instances
 shared one definition and the first to arrive answered for both. Both rows
 must print their own instance's text; if either prints the other's, the
 head's type argument is being discarded again.

 `plain-integer` is the control: a nullary head, whose key must stay
 exactly what it always was, so a regression that broke ordinary instances
 shows here rather than hiding.

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("plain-integer  : " & to-text 7)
    print-line-uni ("compound-int   : " & to-text [1, 2, 3])
    print-line-uni ("compound-text  : " & to-text ["a", "b"])
  end

